  "question": {
    "text": "A neutral sphere of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math> is placed in a uniform electric field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;that exists&nbsp;on the earth&#39;s surface in the vertically upward direction.&nbsp;If the atomic number and the mass number of the material of the sphere are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;respectively, then the&nbsp;fraction of electrons that should be removed from the sphere for it to remain in equilibrium is [Assume that the sphere is able to hold the necessary charge without any leakage. Here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;-- Avogadro number]&nbsp;"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>total</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x3C1;</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>",
